## CKA Exam Drills

### Drill 1: Inline NFS Volume Mount

Directly mount an NFS share in a Pod without pre-creating a PersistentVolume:

```bash
kubectl apply -f 01-nfs/01-nfs-direct-pod.yaml
kubectl get pod nfs-direct-pod
kubectl exec nfs-direct-pod -- cat /mnt/nfs/index.html
kubectl exec nfs-direct-pod -- cat /mnt/nfs/shared-data.txt
```

### Drill 2: Multi-Replica Shared Storage (ReadWriteMany)

Deploy multiple replicas of an Nginx web server mounting the same NFS directory. Both replicas serve the identical `index.html` from the NFS server:

```bash
kubectl apply -f 01-nfs/02-nfs-deployment-shared.yaml
kubectl rollout status deployment/nfs-shared-web
kubectl get pods -l app=nfs-shared-web -o wide
# Check logs or curl pod IPs
```

### Drill 3: Static PersistentVolume & PersistentVolumeClaim Lifecycle

Practice binding a PersistentVolume to a PersistentVolumeClaim and attaching it to a Pod:

```bash
# 1. Create the PersistentVolume
kubectl apply -f 01-nfs/03-nfs-pv.yaml
kubectl get pv nfs-storage-pv

# 2. Create the PersistentVolumeClaim and verify Bound status
kubectl apply -f 01-nfs/04-nfs-pvc.yaml
kubectl get pvc nfs-storage-pvc

# 3. Mount PVC into Pod
kubectl apply -f 01-nfs/05-nfs-pvc-pod.yaml
kubectl get pod nfs-pvc-consumer
kubectl exec nfs-pvc-consumer -- cat /data/shared/shared-data.txt
```

### Drill 4: Multi-Container emptyDir Sharing

Demonstrate the sidecar pattern where an init/app container writes data and a companion container reads from the shared `emptyDir`:

```bash
kubectl apply -f 02-emptydir/01-emptydir-sidecar-pod.yaml
kubectl logs emptydir-sidecar-pod -c reader-sidecar
```

### Drill 5: Automated Verification

Run the test script to automatically validate all NFS mounts, DNS resolution, and read/write operations:

```bash
./test-nfs-mounts.sh
```
